From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: secur32: DecryptMessage's second missing buffer says how many bytes are missing

Given part of a TLS record, DecryptMessage returns SEC_E_INCOMPLETE_MESSAGE
and marks two buffers SECBUFFER_MISSING: the data buffer and the first
empty one (pBuffers[1] in the usual four). The first said how many bytes
were still to come; the second, computed from its own size (0), said the
whole record's size. Rust's schannel crate (reqwest's native TLS: Discord's
updater, among others) reads the count from pBuffers[1] and then reads
until that many more bytes have arrived -- more than the server will ever
send at the end of a response. Every download stalled on its last record
until the server dropped the idle connection, minutes later: Discord's
first start took seven minutes, and every start after it several.

Both buffers now carry the missing count, as on Windows (where
InitializeSecurityContext's conformance tests see the same in
pBuffers[1]); an array with no empty buffer is no longer indexed at -1.

Gate: test/commapps-gate.sh.
---
--- a/dlls/secur32/schannel.c
+++ b/dlls/secur32/schannel.c
@@ -1515,15 +1515,24 @@
     expected_size = ctx->header_size + read_record_size(buf_ptr, ctx->header_size);
     if(buffer->cbBuffer < expected_size)
     {
+        ULONG missing = expected_size - buffer->cbBuffer;
+
         TRACE("Expected %u bytes, but buffer only contains %lu bytes\n", expected_size, buffer->cbBuffer);
         buffer->BufferType = SECBUFFER_MISSING;
-        buffer->cbBuffer = expected_size - buffer->cbBuffer;
+        buffer->cbBuffer = missing;
 
-        /* This is a bit weird, but windows does it too */
+        /* This is a bit weird, but windows does it too. The empty buffer
+         * says how many bytes are missing as well: callers read the count
+         * from either (Rust's schannel crate from pBuffers[1]), and one
+         * that was told the whole record's size waits for bytes that never
+         * come. */
         idx = schan_find_sec_buffer_idx(message, 0, SECBUFFER_EMPTY);
-        buffer = &message->pBuffers[idx];
-        buffer->BufferType = SECBUFFER_MISSING;
-        buffer->cbBuffer = expected_size - buffer->cbBuffer;
+        if (idx != -1)
+        {
+            buffer = &message->pBuffers[idx];
+            buffer->BufferType = SECBUFFER_MISSING;
+            buffer->cbBuffer = missing;
+        }
 
         TRACE("Returning SEC_E_INCOMPLETE_MESSAGE\n");
         return SEC_E_INCOMPLETE_MESSAGE;
